# Cursor/AI Agent Rules for Lazy Export

This file helps AI coding assistants (Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Claude, etc.) understand this codebase and provide better suggestions.

## Project Context

**Name:** Lazy Export
**Type:** Figma Plugin
**Version:** 2.0.0
**Stack:** React 18 + TypeScript 5 + Vite 7 + Figma Plugin API v1.0.0

**Purpose:**
A Figma plugin that allows users to quickly apply export settings to selected layers using preset configurations. Supports iOS, Android, Web, and PDF exports with custom preset creation.

## Architecture Overview

### Dual-Context Plugin
- **UI Layer**: React app in iframe (`src/ui/`) - has DOM access, no Figma API
- **Plugin Layer**: Sandbox code (`src/plugin/`) - has Figma API, no DOM access
- **Communication**: postMessage API between layers
- **Shared Code**: Types and constants in `src/shared/`

### Critical Constraints
1. **Never access `figma` object in UI code** - It doesn't exist there
2. **Never access `window`/`document` in plugin code** - Sandbox has no DOM
3. **Always use postMessage for communication** - Only way to pass data between layers
4. **Use Figma CSS variables for all colors** - Required for Dark Mode support

## Code Style Preferences

### TypeScript
- **Always use strict mode** - No `any`, use `unknown` + type guards
- **Explicit return types** on all functions
- **Interface over type** for object shapes
- **Discriminated unions** for message types

### React
- **Functional components only** - No class components
- **TypeScript for all props** - Define interfaces for every component
- **Hooks** - useState, useEffect, useMemo, useCallback as needed
- **Controlled components** for forms

### CSS
- **Use Figma CSS variables** - `var(--figma-color-bg)` not hex codes
- **BEM naming convention** - `component__element--modifier`
- **One CSS file per component** - Component.tsx + Component.css
- **Mobile-first** thinking (though plugin is fixed-width)

### Naming Conventions
- **Components**: PascalCase (`PresetCard.tsx`)
- **Functions**: camelCase (`applyPreset()`)
- **Constants**: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE (`DEFAULT_PRESETS`)
- **CSS classes**: kebab-case with BEM (`preset-card__icon`)

## File Organization

```
src/
├── ui/                  # React app (browser context)
│   ├── App.tsx          # Main component
│   ├── main.tsx         # React entry point
│   ├── components/      # Reusable components
│   └── styles/          # Global CSS
├── plugin/              # Plugin code (Figma context)
│   └── main.ts          # Plugin entry point
└── shared/              # Shared between UI and plugin
    ├── types.ts         # TypeScript definitions
    └── presets.ts       # Default preset configs
```

## Common Patterns

### Adding a New Message Type

1. Define in `src/shared/types.ts`:
```typescript
export type UIMessage =
  | { type: 'new-action'; data: string }
  | ...existing types;
```

2. Send from UI (`src/ui/App.tsx`):
```typescript
const handleAction = () => {
  const message: UIMessage = { type: 'new-action', data: 'value' };
  parent.postMessage({ pluginMessage: message }, '*');
};
```

3. Handle in plugin (`src/plugin/main.ts`):
```typescript
figma.ui.onmessage = (msg: UIMessage) => {
  if (msg.type === 'new-action') {
    // Handle the action
  }
};
```

### Adding a New UI Component

1. Create files:
   - `src/ui/components/MyComponent.tsx`
   - `src/ui/components/MyComponent.css`

2. Component template:
```typescript
import React from 'react';
import './MyComponent.css';

interface MyComponentProps {
  title: string;
  onClick: () => void;
}

export const MyComponent: React.FC<MyComponentProps> = ({ title, onClick }) => {
  return (
    <button className="my-component" onClick={onClick}>
      {title}
    </button>
  );
};
```

3. CSS template:
```css
.my-component {
  background-color: var(--figma-color-bg);
  border: 1px solid var(--figma-color-border);
  color: var(--figma-color-text);
}

.my-component:hover {
  background-color: var(--figma-color-bg-hover);
}
```

### Adding a New Default Preset

Edit `src/shared/presets.ts`:
```typescript
export const DEFAULT_PRESETS: PresetConfig[] = [
  ...existing presets,
  {
    id: 'new-preset',
    name: 'New Preset',
    platform: 'Web',
    icon: '🎨',
    settings: [
      { format: 'PNG', suffix: '@2x', constraint: { type: 'SCALE', value: 2 } },
    ],
  },
];
```

## Important Files and Their Purpose

| File | Purpose | Can Access |
|------|---------|------------|
| `src/ui/App.tsx` | Main React component, state management | DOM, React, postMessage |
| `src/plugin/main.ts` | Plugin logic, Figma API interactions | Figma API, clientStorage |
| `src/shared/types.ts` | TypeScript definitions for entire project | Both contexts |
| `src/shared/presets.ts` | Default preset configurations | Both contexts |
| `vite.config.ts` | UI build configuration | N/A (build time) |
| `build-plugin.js` | Plugin build configuration (esbuild) | N/A (build time) |

## Available Figma CSS Variables

Use these instead of hardcoded colors:

### Backgrounds
- `--figma-color-bg` - Main background
- `--figma-color-bg-hover` - Hover state
- `--figma-color-bg-pressed` - Active/pressed state
- `--figma-color-bg-secondary` - Secondary background
- `--figma-color-bg-brand` - Brand color (blue)
- `--figma-color-bg-danger` - Destructive actions (red)

### Text
- `--figma-color-text` - Primary text
- `--figma-color-text-secondary` - Secondary text
- `--figma-color-text-tertiary` - Tertiary text
- `--figma-color-text-onbrand` - Text on brand backgrounds
- `--figma-color-text-ondanger` - Text on danger backgrounds

### Borders
- `--figma-color-border` - Default border
- `--figma-color-border-strong` - Emphasized border
- `--figma-color-border-selected` - Selected/focused state

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

### ❌ Don't Do This
```typescript
// Hardcoded colors
.button { background: #fff; }

// Accessing figma in UI
const selection = figma.currentPage.selection; // ❌ figma is undefined

// Accessing DOM in plugin
const button = document.querySelector('.btn'); // ❌ document is undefined

// Using 'any' type
function handle(data: any) { } // ❌ Lose type safety

// Missing return type
function calculate(x: number) { } // ❌ What does it return?
```

### ✅ Do This Instead
```typescript
// Figma CSS variables
.button { background: var(--figma-color-bg); }

// postMessage to get selection
parent.postMessage({ pluginMessage: { type: 'get-selection' } }, '*');

// Send message to UI
figma.ui.postMessage({ type: 'update', data: 'value' });

// Proper typing
function handle(data: unknown): void {
  if (typeof data === 'string') { }
}

// Explicit return type
function calculate(x: number): number {
  return x * 2;
}
```

## Testing Workflow

1. **Make changes to code**
2. **Run build**: `npm run build`
3. **In Figma**:
   - Plugins → Development → Reload plugin (or re-import if manifest changed)
4. **Test manually** using checklist in CONTRIBUTING.md
5. **Check DevTools**:
   - UI: Right-click → Inspect Element
   - Plugin: Plugins → Development → Open Console

## Performance Tips

- **Use React.memo** for expensive components
- **Use useMemo** for derived state
- **Use useCallback** for event handlers passed to children
- **Avoid inline object creation** in render (causes re-renders)
- **Keep bundle size under 200kb** (currently ~176kb)

## Security Reminders

- **No network access** - Plugin has `networkAccess: none` in manifest
- **Validate all user input** - Never trust data from forms
- **Use TypeScript** - Catch errors at compile time
- **No eval()** - Never use eval or Function constructor

## Build Commands

```bash
npm run build          # Production build (both UI and plugin)
npm run build:plugin   # Build plugin only
npm run dev            # Development mode with watch
```

## When Making Suggestions

### Prefer:
- Functional React components with hooks
- TypeScript interfaces over types (for objects)
- Discriminated unions for message types
- CSS variables over hardcoded colors
- Explicit return types
- Descriptive variable names

### Avoid:
- Class components
- `any` type
- Hardcoded hex colors
- Abbreviations in names
- Large components (split into smaller ones)
- Mixing concerns (UI code in plugin, vice versa)

